WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.320
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:10.400
Today we are going to talk a bit about the
glory and the woes of cloth simulation programs.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.039
In these simulations, we have several 3D models
of garments that are built from up to several

00:00:15.039 --> 00:00:17.790
hundreds of thousands of triangles.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:22.670
And as they move in time, the interesting
part of the simulation is whenever collisions

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:29.060
happen, however, evaluating how these meshes
collide is quite difficult and time consuming.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:34.010
Basically, we have to tell an algorithm that
we have piece of cloth with a hundred thousand

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:40.460
connected triangles here, and another one
there, and now, have a look and tell me which

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:45.780
collides with which and how they bend and
change in response these forces.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.690
And don’t forget about friction and repulsive
forces!

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:55.370
Also, please, be accurate because every small
error adds up over time, and, do it several

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:59.639
times a second so we can have a look at the
results interactively.

00:00:59.639 --> 00:01:02.870
Well, this is a quite challenging problem.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:10.030
And it takes quite long to compute, so much
so that 70-80% of the total time taken to

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:13.630
perform the simulation is spent with doing
collision handling.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.700
So how can we make it not take forever?

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.979
Well, one way would be to try to make sure
that we can run this collision handling step

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:23.660
on the graphics card.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:28.579
This is exactly what this work does, and in
order to do this, we have to make sure that

00:01:28.579 --> 00:01:32.620
all these evaluations can be performed in
parallel.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.479
Of course, this is easier said than done.

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:38.869
Another difficulty is choosing the appropriate
time steps.

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:44.590
These simulations are run in a way that we
check and resolve all of the collisions, and

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:49.439
then, we can advance the simulation forward
by a tiny amount.

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:54.679
This amount of called a time step, and choosing
the appropriate time step has always been

00:01:54.679 --> 00:01:55.969
a challenge.

00:01:55.969 --> 00:02:01.939
You see, if we set it to too large, we will
be done faster and compute less, however,

00:02:01.939 --> 00:02:06.700
we will almost certainly miss some collisions
because we skipped over them.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:12.120
The simulation may end up in a state that
is so incorrect that it is impossible to recover

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.570
from, and we have to throw the entire thing
out.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:21.690
If we set it to too low, we get a more robust
simulation, however, it will take many hours

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.819
to days to compute.

00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:29.400
To remedy this, this technique is built in
a way such that we can use larger time steps.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.220
That’s excellent news.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:36.769
Also, the collision computation part is now
up to 9 times faster, and if we look at the

00:02:36.769 --> 00:02:41.870
cloth simulation as a whole, that can be made
over 3 times faster.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:47.430
As you see here, this is especially nice because
we can test how these garments react to our

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:51.129
manipulations at 8-10 frames per second.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:55.500
If you have a closer look at the paper, you
will also find another key observation which

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.290
states that most of the time, only a small
subregion of the simulated cloth undergoes

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:07.129
deformation due to response forces, and this
knowledge can be kept track of, which contributed

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:10.129
to cutting down the simulation time significantly.

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:13.329
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

